const faqs = [
  { question: 'What type of marine diesel engine is best for a sailing yacht?', answer: 'Most displacement sailing yachts use a compact inboard auxiliary with a shaft or saildrive. The correct choice depends on installed dimensions, displacement, propeller match, charging needs, service access and local support—not brand alone.' },
  { question: 'Is a sterndrive a type of engine?', answer: 'No. A sterndrive is the steerable drive behind the engine. Engine and drive are sold as a matched propulsion package, but the drive has separate corrosion, bellows, oil and haul-out requirements.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between recreational and commercial marine engine ratings?', answer: 'The rating states the permitted load factor, annual hours and time at high output. Two versions of the same engine family can carry different ratings, so maximum horsepower alone cannot establish suitability.' },
  { question: 'Are marinised industrial engines suitable for boats?', answer: 'Yes when the complete marinisation and duty rating suit the vessel. The base engine may be industrial, while the mariniser supplies the heat exchanger, wet exhaust, mounts, controls and other marine-specific systems.' },
  { question: 'Should I compare engines by horsepower?', answer: 'Horsepower is only one input. Compare duty rating, torque and propeller match, installed dimensions, weight, gearbox ratio, cooling and exhaust requirements, electrical integration, service access and Australian support.' },
];

